  "profile": {
    "video_id": "kenji:-riGSANPe3g",
    "purpose": "Shows how to make a five-ingredient Colombian-style chicken, potato and tomato stew in a pressure cooker with no added liquid, and explains why pressure cooking speeds things up.",
    "video_type": "how_to",
    "thesis": "A pressure cooker turns raw bone-in chicken, potatoes, tomato, onion and bay leaves into a concentrated, brothy stew in about 25 minutes, because higher pressure raises water's boiling point and speeds cooking.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Ingredients are bone-in, skin-on chicken pieces, Yukon Gold potatoes, tomato, onion wedges and bay leaves, all added raw and seasoned with salt and pepper.",
      "No liquid is added; the water released by the chicken and vegetables builds the steam and pressure.",
      "Cooked at high pressure (12 psi on the Breville) for 15 minutes after reaching pressure; Kenji says the written recipe's 25 minutes is overkill.",
      "He used a quick release; stovetop cookers can be cooled under cold water in the sink.",
      "Pressure raises the boiling point to roughly 240-250°F, which he says makes 15 minutes equal to about an hour of normal braising.",
      "Browning the chicken first drives off moisture, so a cup or two of chicken broth would be needed to build pressure.",
      "The broth can be reduced to thicken it, but Kenji likes it brothy.",
      "Kenji learned the dish from his Colombian wife, whose family lives in high-altitude Bogotá, where pressure cookers are common."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
